#---
# purpose: "atom-keyed map iteration uses the same name order as the language term order"
#---
#
# The allocation snapshot is part of the ordering contract. Comparing atom
# names must not materialize fz strings or otherwise change the map/list shape
# of these operations. The pre-fix and post-fix snapshots are byte-identical:
#
# - native: map `0/0`, list cons `34/544`, struct `3/96`, scalar box `7/112`;
# - interpreter: map `3/144`, list cons `30/480`, struct `3/96`, scalar box
#   `10/160`.
#
# Each pair is `allocs/bytes`. Native escape analysis keeps the maps out of the
# process heap; the interpreter materializes them. The fixed atom-list input to
# `Enum.sort/1` is deliberate: feeding it `Map.keys/1` would change merge-sort's
# work-list shape when the bug is fixed and would confound comparator cost with
# a different input order.
fn main() do
  map = %{z: 1, a: 2, m: 3}

  dbg(Kernel.compare(:z, :a))
  dbg(Enum.sort([:z, :a, :m]))
  dbg(Map.keys(map))
  dbg(Map.values(map))
  dbg(Map.to_list(map))

  stats = Process.heap_alloc_stats()
  dbg({
    stats[:map_allocs],
    stats[:map_bytes],
    stats[:list_cons_allocs],
    stats[:list_cons_bytes],
    stats[:struct_allocs],
    stats[:struct_bytes],
    stats[:scalar_box_allocs],
    stats[:scalar_box_bytes]
  })
end
